10. Character Generation
Character generation extracts or creates important characters.
Each character should have a persistent profile:
Character
├── Name
├── Age
├── Role
├── Personality
├── Appearance
├── Clothing
├── Background
└── Relationships
This information can later be reused whenever Gemini generates a new scene or visual prompt.
________________________________________
11. Scene Development
The story is divided into logical scenes.
Example:
Scene 1 – Discovery
Scene 2 – Robot Activation
Scene 3 – First Adventure
Scene 4 – Conflict
Scene 5 – Resolution
Each scene contains:
•	Location
•	Time
•	Characters
•	Events
•	Mood
•	Description
________________________________________
12. Panel Generation
Each scene is divided into comic panels.
Example:
Scene 1
│
├── Panel 1
│   ├── Visual description
│   ├── Dialogue
│   └── Narration
│
├── Panel 2
│   ├── Visual description
│   ├── Dialogue
│   └── Narration
│
└── Panel 3
    ├── Visual description
    ├── Dialogue
    └── Narration
Users should be able to regenerate a single panel rather than the complete comic.
________________________________________
13. Dialogue Development
Dialogue is generated according to:
•	Character personality
•	Current scene
•	Story context
•	Emotional state
•	Genre
•	Intended audience
The editor should allow users to directly modify AI-generated dialogue.
________________________________________
14. Visual Generation
The panel information is converted into a visual prompt.
Example
Scene:
A teenager enters an abandoned laboratory.

Characters:
Arun – 17-year-old boy, black hair,
blue jacket, backpack.

Action:
Arun discovers a damaged robot.

Mood:
Mysterious and suspenseful.

Environment:
Dark futuristic laboratory.
This visual prompt can then be passed to the selected image-generation service.
